edb/ref 17592186479717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479822 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479822 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479822 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479822 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f974a2a4-9c4f-5a1b-821e-f099326de82a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479828 #edb/ref 1071 "If $-5=\\frac{t}{4},$ then $t=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "063f6e3f-2db2-5183-9271-70afe486d5c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479828 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\frac{t}{4}=-5\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $t$ is being divided by $4.$ To isolate $t$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $4.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{4} & =-5 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{t}{4} & =4\\cdot (-5) \\\\ \\frac{4t}{4} & =-20 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}t}{\\cancel{4}} & =-20 \\\\ t & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479831 #edb/ref 1071 "If $-\\frac{x}{7}=-3,$ then $x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b5cb4d7-77e4-5ac4-8439-cb36ef6f1c98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479831 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{7} & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{7}) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{x}{7} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $7.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $7$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7} & =3 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{x}{7} & =7\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & =21 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479844 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479844 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479844 #edb/ref 1071 "If $-8x=-56$, then $x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3418ccb3-fdc3-5d51-a5fe-8860e2e0eae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479844 #edb/ref 1074 "The variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $-8.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-8.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-8.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8x & =-56 \\\\ \\frac{-8x}{-8} & =\\frac{-56}{-8} \\\\ \\frac{-8x}{-8} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8x}{-8} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-8}x}{\\cancel{-8}} & =7 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479855 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479855 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2529" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479855 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479855 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{y}{3}=-7,$ then $y=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479855 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "559e6eeb-8cf0-577b-a9ee-88ad70f4ec15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479855 #edb/ref 1074 "In the equation, $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =-7 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & =3\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & =-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{3} & =-21 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & =-21 \\\\ y & =-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479865 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479865 #edb/ref 1069 "q-185208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479865 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479865 #edb/ref 1071 "If $4=\\frac{x}{6},$ then $x=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479865 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ce50429-066c-52cc-b521-7f9c1d989259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479865 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}=4\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is being divided by $6.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $6.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6} & =4 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{x}{6} & =6\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{6x}{6} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}x}{\\cancel{6}} & =24 \\\\ x & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479882 #edb/ref 1071 "If $-\\frac{c}{12}=4,$ then $c=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4c0e9de-946c-5a43-9beb-5626829b00ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479882 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{c}{12} & =4 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{c}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{c}{12} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $c$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $c$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{12} & =-4 \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{c}{12} & =12\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{12c}{12} & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}c}{\\cancel{12}} & =-48 \\\\ c & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479891 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479891 #edb/ref 1071 "If $-5t=-60$, then $t=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1c0c05b-9184-5c94-9e0b-29a6b0d0aa2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479891 #edb/ref 1074 "The variable $t$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $t$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5t & =-60 \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5} & =\\frac{-60}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5t}{-5} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}t}{\\cancel{-5}} & =12 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-101703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479893 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\frac{c}{7}=-15,$ then $c=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d37f3cd3-5a50-54b2-83fb-c1289723c204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479893 #edb/ref 1074 "In the equation, $c$ is being divided by $7.$ To isolate $c$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $7.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{7} & =-15 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{c}{7} & =7\\cdot (-15) \\\\ \\frac{7c}{7} & =-105\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7c}{7} & =-105 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}c}{\\cancel{7}} & =-105 \\\\ c & =-105\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
